<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Pink Flowers! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Our Garden.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Pink Flowers!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name primary and secondary colors. Interactive: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify and name colors of familiar nature objects (e.g., "green trees," "pink flowers," or "yellow leaves"). Can respond to simple questions about nature with words or gestures (e.g., "What is this?" Student points and says, "tree."). Can express likes or dislikes about nature-related objects using simple words and pointing (e.g., "I like trees" while pointing to a tree).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification of the lesson's theme and key vocabulary, building on prior knowledge from Lessons 1 & 2.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Hello Song" with actions. Then, the teacher holds up large, colorful flashcards of garden items (flower, tree, leaf, bush, sun) and colors (pink, green, yellow). The teacher points to each and says, "What is this? It's a flower! What color is it? It's pink!" Students echo the names of objects and colors.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a large picture of a garden (from previous lessons) and explicitly models sentences: "This is a tree. The tree is green." (using /g/ and /t/ pronunciation). "These are flowers. The flowers are pink." The teacher points to a specific item and uses a simple gesture for 'like' and 'don't like': "I like pink flowers!" (thumbs up), "I don't like brown leaves." (thumbs down, even if not explicitly taught, it introduces the structure).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and gestures to check understanding.
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a flower?" (pointing to a flower). (Expected answer: "Yes!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color is this?" (pointing to a pink flower). (Expected answer: "Pink!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks students to point to "the green tree" or "the small plant" to ensure recognition.</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "It's Our Garden." through visual and auditory means, preparing for active speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher displays a large, colorful "Our Garden" poster/chart featuring various plants, flowers, trees, and the sun, with clear examples of pink flowers and green trees. Each item is clearly visible.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large garden picture chart/poster, flashcards of garden vocabulary (flower, tree, plant, leaf, bush, sun, soil) and colors (pink, green).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will interact orally to exchange simple information about familiar objects in nature, using basic descriptions and questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Choral Response):</b> The teacher points to elements on the garden poster and asks, "What is this?" (e.g., "tree," "flower," "leaf"). Students respond chorally. The teacher ensures students pronounce /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree" correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"What color?" Questions (Individual Response):</b> The teacher points to a specific item and asks, "What color is the flower?" or "What color is the tree?" Individual students raise their hands to respond: "Pink!" or "Green!" The teacher also asks, "Is the sun green? True or False?" (False!).</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking. Students practice using the target language in controlled and semi-controlled activities to build confidence and correct production.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Noun + verb + adjective ("The flowers are pink."), WH-questions ("What color is the flower?"), Present simple ("This is a tall tree.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> garden, tree, flower, plant, leaf, bush, sun, pink, green, tall, short, small, big, what, this, that.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /g/ in "garden," /t/ in "tree," /s/ in "sun."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Describing plants/trees, asking about colors.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Picture Says":</b> The teacher holds up flashcards of garden items (e.g., a green tree, a pink flower). The teacher first says the full sentence "This is a green tree." and students repeat chorally. Then, "The flowers are pink." Students repeat. The teacher then prompts, holding up a flashcard, and students say the complete sentence. For example, Teacher: (holds up pink flower) "The flowers are..." Students: "Pink!" (Teacher confirms: "The flowers are pink!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – "Color Match & Say":</b> Students receive small individual cards with either a garden item (flower, tree, leaf) or a color (pink, green). The teacher calls out a combination, e.g., "Pink flower!" Students with matching cards stand up and say, "I have a pink flower." or "The flower is pink." The teacher then points to a student's card and asks, "What color is your flower?" The student replies, "My flower is pink."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Ask and Point":</b> Students work in pairs with a shared garden poster or a set of small flashcards. Student A points to an item and asks, "What is this?" or "What color is the flower?" Student B answers, "It is a tree." or "It is pink." Then, Student B asks Student A. Encourage simple expressions of likes/dislikes: "I like pink flowers." or "I like green trees." while pointing. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, especially /g/ in "garden" and /t/ in "tree."</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency, collaborative interaction, and task completion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task:</b> "Our Garden Talk-Around"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students) or in pairs. Each group is given a large "mini-garden" drawing or a collection of realia (small plants, artificial flowers, leaves). Students take turns pointing to and describing the garden elements, asking and answering questions about their colors, and expressing what they like or dislike.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides the class into small groups or pairs. Each group gets a "mini-garden" set-up (could be a large printout, flashcards, or actual small plants/flowers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A points to an item (e.g., a flower) and says, "This is a flower. It is pink." or "The flower is pink."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B asks, "What color is the leaf?" Student C responds, "It is green."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student D (or A again) then expresses a preference: "I like pink flowers." or "I like tall trees." (while pointing to the object).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rotate turns, ensuring everyone gets a chance to speak, ask, and answer.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing, listening for the target language (Noun + verb + adjective, WH-questions, "I like/don't like"), and offering gentle prompts ("What about the bush?" "Do you like it?"). The teacher encourages respectful language when talking about nature.</li>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning, followed by student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student performance during the "Our Garden Talk-Around" activity or a brief individual check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student will be called upon individually or in small groups (briefly) to point to 1-2 garden items (on the poster or flashcards), name its color, and respond to one "What color...?" question, or state a like/dislike. For example, Teacher: "What is this?" (points to a flower). Student: "It's a flower. It's pink." Teacher: "Do you like green trees?" Student: "Yes, I like green trees."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of garden vocabulary (e.g., /g/ in "garden," /t/ in "tree") and colors.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Accurately uses "flower," "tree," "leaf," "pink," "green," "what," "this."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> Can form simple sentences like "The flowers are pink," "What color is...?", "I like..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Attempts to participate in the "Talk-Around" by asking and answering questions.</li>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show a thumbs up if they can "say 'pink flower'" and a thumbs up if they can "ask 'What color is it?'" They can also point to a picture of a flower and say "I like pink flowers" if they feel confident.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Guiding students in reflecting on their learning and the teacher's self-assessment for future lesson adjustments.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you say today about our garden?" (e.g., "pink," "flower," "tree").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like to talk about in our garden?" (e.g., "pink flowers," "green trees").</li>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? (e.g., TPR, pair work with visuals, repetition of "I like" statements).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., more structured sentence frames for some students, more varied question types, better management of group speaking turns).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did all students meet the speaking learning outcomes? How can I support those who struggled with specific grammar or vocabulary?</li>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great job speaking about our garden today! Next time, we will learn how to write sentences about our garden and our pink flowers!" (This sets the stage for Lesson 4: Writing Composition).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>